---
title: Summer Pudding
description: Classic British dessert
source: Le Cordon Bleu Dessert Techniques <urn:isbn:0304351202>
author: Laurent Duchene & Bridget Jones
servings: 12
course: Dessert
locale: en_GB
difficulty: Hard
prep time: 1h
cook time: 10m
cuisine: British
diet: Vegetarian
tags:
- british
- vegetarian
- dessert
- pudding
- sweet
---

Line a #deep 1.25 litre pudding basin{} with the @bread, crusts removed{}. Begin with a circle of bread cut to the right size for the base, then overlap slices evenly around the side.

Place the @blackcurrants and redcurrants{250%g} in a pan with the @sugar{150%g}. Add @water{2%tbsp} and cook gently, stirring occasionally until the sugar has dissolved and the juice runs from the fruit. Simmer for ~{5%minutes} then remove from the heat.

Cut any large strawberries into halves or quarters, then add them to the currants with the other @mixed berries{250%g}(such as strawberries, raspberries, mulberries and blueberries).

Spoon some of the juice from the mixture into the basin lined with bread, then fill it with the fruit, pressing the filling down well.

Fold down the bread lining the sides and top with an even layer of bread. If there is any juice remaining, spoon this over the top layer of bread.

Stand the basin in a dish to catch any overspill of juice, cover with a saucer and place a heavy weight on top. Chill overnight.

Invert on to a flat platter and remove the basin just before serving. If there is any juice pooled on the dish, spoon it over the pudding, particularly over any bread which has not soaked up the juice. Decorate with fresh berries.